<Page
	title="About Us"
	description="SpaceNews is an independent space news publication covering missions, technology, astronomy, and exploration — how we choose stories, verify facts, and correct mistakes."
	eyebrow="Who We Are"
	updated="November 8, 2026"
>
	<p>
		<strong>SpaceNews</strong> is an independent space news publication dedicated to one of
		humanity's greatest adventures: the exploration of space. From rocket launches and space
		station operations to deep-space science and the race back to the Moon, we cover the missions,
		machines, and discoveries shaping our future beyond Earth — and we publish new reporting every
		day.
	</p>
	<p>
		Spaceflight moves fast, and it is easy for rumours, outdated schedules, and plain hype to
		outrun the facts. SpaceNews exists to slow that down: to report what has actually happened,
		to explain what is genuinely planned, and to be honest about what is still uncertain. Every
		story we publish is grounded in sources you can check for yourself, and this page explains
		exactly how we work.
	</p>

	<h2>What We Cover</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Missions</strong> — launches, landings, space station operations, and deep-space
			voyages, from final countdown checks to confirmed results.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Technology</strong> — rockets, spacecraft, satellites, spacesuits, and the
			engineering behind them.
		</li>
		<li><strong>Astronomy</strong> — telescopes, exoplanets, galaxies, and cosmic discoveries.</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Exploration</strong> — the Moon, Mars, and humanity's long-term future in space.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		Within those beats we favour stories with a verifiable core: a launch that has happened or is
		officially scheduled, a peer-reviewed result, an agency announcement, a mission milestone.
		Alongside breaking coverage we publish background explainers that put a single event in its
		wider context — the history of a programme, the physics behind a discovery, the track record
		of a rocket — because a launch is more meaningful when you know what came before it.
	</p>

	<h2>Who Publishes SpaceNews</h2>
	<p>
		SpaceNews is an independent online publication, published under the SpaceNews name at
		SpaceNews.biz.id. We are not owned by, affiliated with, or endorsed by any space agency,
		launch provider, or aerospace company, and no outside organisation directs our coverage. The
		publisher is editorially responsible for everything that appears on this site and can be
		reached at <a href="mailto:SpaceNews@info.com">SpaceNews@info.com</a> or through our
		<a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
	</p>
	<p>
		Our articles are published under the SpaceNews name rather than under individual bylines. That
		is a deliberate choice: we will never invent staff profiles, biographies, or credentials to
		make this publication look like something it is not. What we can show you instead is our
		process — how stories are chosen, checked, and corrected — which is set out below and in full
		on our <a href="/editorial-policy">Editorial Policy</a> page.
	</p>

	<h2>How We Choose Stories</h2>
	<p>
		Every story starts with a simple test: has something verifiable happened, or is something
		verifiable about to happen? Confirmed events come first — a liftoff, a landing, a docking, a
		published study. Scheduled events are covered as previews, clearly labelled as such, and
		followed up with result coverage once the outcome is known. If a launch slips, the preview is
		not quietly recycled as news; the schedule change itself becomes part of the story.
	</p>
	<p>
		We also ask whether a story can be sourced properly. If the only evidence for a claim is an
		anonymous social media post or a single unsourced report, we either wait for confirmation or
		say plainly that the claim is unconfirmed. Some stories we deliberately do not chase: leaked
		details we cannot verify, speculative timelines presented as fact, and announcements of
		announcements. When a topic is genuinely uncertain — a classified mission, an early scientific
		result — we report what is known, identify what is analysis or assessment rather than official
		fact, and leave the rest open.
	</p>

	<h2>How We Verify</h2>
	<p>
		Our reporting is built on primary sources wherever possible: space agency and company mission
		pages, official statements, peer-reviewed papers, and mission data. Key facts — dates, times,
		figures, names — are checked against those sources before publication, and launch times in
		particular are re-verified on the day, because schedules move. Every article ends with a
		Sources section linking to the material it is based on, so you can check our work yourself.
	</p>
	<p>
		We are careful with the difference between what has happened, what is planned, and what is
		merely hoped for. A target date is not a launch; a plan is not a result. Where officials have
		not confirmed a detail — the number of satellites on a classified flight, for example — we do
		not present estimates as confirmation, and we say who the estimate comes from when we cite
		one.
	</p>

	<h2>How Our Articles Are Made</h2>
	<p>
		SpaceNews uses modern research and drafting tools, including AI-assisted tools, as part of its
		production process. Those tools work within standards set by the publisher: topics must pass
		the story test above, every factual claim must trace back to a cited source, and an article
		that cannot be verified against its sources is not published. Automation helps us cover a
		fast-moving beat daily; it does not lower the bar for accuracy, and it never invents sources,
		quotations, or events. The full process is described on our
		<a href="/editorial-policy">Editorial Policy</a> page.
	</p>

	<h2>Corrections</h2>
	<p>
		When we get something wrong, we correct it promptly and transparently: significant fixes are
		noted on the article itself, and our <a href="/corrections">Corrections page</a> explains how
		to report an error and how we handle it. If you spot a mistake, please
		<a href="/contact">let us know</a> — reader corrections are one of the best checks we have.
	</p>

	<h2>Images</h2>
	<p>
		Photography on SpaceNews comes from <a href="https://unsplash.com" rel="noopener">Unsplash</a>,
		unless otherwise credited. Images are illustrative — they may not depict the specific mission,
		vehicle, or event described — and every image carries a caption that says so honestly.
	</p>

	<h2>Advertising and Independence</h2>
	<p>
		SpaceNews is funded by advertising, including ads served by Google AdSense. Advertising never
		determines what we cover or how we cover it, advertisers have no advance sight of our
		articles, and sponsored material is never disguised as news. Our
		<a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy Policy</a> explains how advertising cookies work and how to
		opt out.
	</p>

	<h2>Get in Touch</h2>
	<p>
		Have a news tip, a correction, or just want to talk about space? We'd love to hear from you —
		head over to our <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
	</p>
</Page>
